@page
@{
	ViewData["Title"] = "";
	ViewData["Description"] = "Almost 100 years of off-roaders: the story of the Mitsubishi Pajero from the PX-33 to the production Pajero — a fan page about Japan's most famous off-road vehicle.";
}

	<div class="text-center">
		<h1>
			The Mitsubishi Pajero
		</h1>
		<figure class="figure">
			<img alt="Almost 100 years of off-road vehicles" src="~/images/history/Ahnenreihe.jpg" class="img-thumbnail" />
			<figcaption>Almost 100 years of off-road vehicles</figcaption>
		</figure>
		<p>
			When the ancestor of the Mitsubishi Pajero was first presented to astonished
			experts at the 1979 Tokyo Motor Show, it was hailed as the company's first
			off-road vehicle. But that wasn't entirely true.
			45 years before that momentous motor show in Tokyo, Mitsubishi could already boast
			of marketing the first series-produced four-wheel-drive passenger car
			and, at the same time, Japan's first off-roader — one that, on top of that, could
			call a diesel engine its own. This legendary vehicle, of which only half an
			original example exists on planet Earth today, goes by the name PX-33.

		</p>
		<figure class="figure">
			<img alt="At first, the Willys Jeep was copied" src="~/images/history/JeepCopy.jpg" class="img-thumbnail" />
			<figcaption>At first, the Willys Jeep was copied</figcaption>
		</figure>
		<p>
			But the car fell into oblivion, and the four-wheel-drive passenger car
			vanished from the scene as quickly as it had appeared. It was not until Audi,
			with its Quattros, made this kind of drivetrain technology socially acceptable again. Yet in
			its day the PX-33 enjoyed the most distinguished admiration. The company chronicles
			hand down the story of Prince Tsunenori Kaya, through whose veins flowed not only blue blood
			but also the stuff that fuels engines. Stories reached the young nobleman's
			ears of a car that could be thrown off course neither by mud nor by steep
			climbs, because it distributed its driving power to all four wheels. The
			requested demonstration was promptly arranged, and so it came about that the technology-loving
			blue-blood experienced up close how Japan's first four-wheel-drive passenger car, spraying wildly in all directions,
			fought its way up a muddy hill. The prince, who had deemed his parade uniform
			the appropriate attire for this occasion, is said to have looked after the demonstration like
			a rally fan in the front row — instead of gleaming white, matte brown.
		</p>
		<figure class="figure">
			<img src="~/images/history/L040_proto.jpg" alt="Prototype" class="img-thumbnail" />
			<figcaption>Prototype 1979</figcaption>
		</figure>
		<figure class="figure">
			<img src="~/images/history/Pajero_82_90.jpg" alt="Testing with the prototype" class="img-thumbnail" />
			<figcaption>Testing with the prototype</figcaption>
		</figure>
		<p>
			But the first four-wheel-drive passenger car was not quite as civilian as it appeared.
			In 1932 the Japanese occupied Manchuria and, in 1933, left the League of Nations over
			disputes about this incident. A year later Japan terminated the Washington
			Naval Treaty, which limited the size of the Japanese naval forces. During that
			time Japan occupied part of northern China, concluded the Anti-Comintern Pact with Germany,
			and, moreover, let the first signs of the war against Chiang Kai-shek flare up. It almost goes
			without saying that the four-wheel-drive sedan was destined to become not a trendsetter but a
			commander's vehicle.
		</p>
		<figure class="figure">
			<img src="~/images/history/PajeroPreSeries.jpg" alt="The pre-production model" class="img-thumbnail" />
			<figcaption>The pre-production model</figcaption>
		</figure>
		<p>
			It was in 1987 that the head of the French import company Sonauto S.A. in St.
			Quen-l’Aumone — Norbert Wagener — became aware of the PX-33 during a company visit
			in Japan. Since the Pajeros were extremely successfully established on the market at that time
			and were shown on television every day in desert rallies, Wagener decided
			to enter a Pajero with a long wheelbase and PX-33
			body in the Paris–Dakar Rally. The rally PX had a 2.6-litre turbo engine and developed
			275 hp, which made it good for 215 km/h. The entire car, with a Kevlar carbon-fibre body,
			weighed just 1250 kilograms and could hold 400 litres of fuel in its innards.
			Wagener offered Mitsubishi Japan the chance to build a small series of 200 units in Japan and,
			after the success of the rally prototype, was confident that he would be able to
			realise his plans. The production model was technically unspectacular. It consisted of the
			underpinnings of the long-wheelbase Pajero and the body of the PX-33 and, with the
			Pajero's 2.6-litre engine of the time, reached a speed of 140 km/h. The impact
			of the car was gigantic, but its realisation got lost in some official's drawer over questions
			of feasibility and financing.
		</p>
		<figure class="figure">
			<img src="~/images/history/PXDakar.jpg" alt="Conversion of an L040 with a PX33 body for the Dakar" class="img-thumbnail" />
			<figcaption>PX33 replica for the Dakar</figcaption>
		</figure>
		<figure class="figure">
			<img src="~/images/history/PX33.jpg" alt="The first Mitsubishi off-road vehicle, the PX33" class="img-thumbnail" />
			<figcaption>The first one. PX33.</figcaption>
		</figure>
		<p>
			The PX-33 may thus have been dead once and for all,
			but the Pajero already existed and enjoyed great popularity. Its story
			begins in 1973, when the Mitsubishi group's first study of a four-wheel-drive off-road
			vehicle, a licence-built version of an American Jeep, was presented to the press
			and trade audience at the Tokyo Motor Show. However, another
			five years passed before the Japanese company bosses gave the go-ahead for series production
			in 1978. In 1979 the ancestor of the production Pajero was unveiled at the Tokyo Motor Show,
			and in 1981 the first production models rolled off the line. It was not until 1983 that the first Mitsubishi
			Pajero L040 rolled off a ship in Europe.
		</p>
		<figure class="figure">
			<img src="~/images/history/PXOriginal.jpg" alt="The last surviving original" class="img-thumbnail" />
			<figcaption>The last surviving original</figcaption>
		</figure>
		<figure class="figure">
			<img src="~/images/history/PXWyn.jpg" alt="The L040/PX33 conversion at the Wyn Rally" class="img-thumbnail" />
			<figcaption>PX33 replica at the Wyn Rally</figcaption>
		</figure>
		<figure class="figure">
			<img alt="The namesake: felis pajero" src="~/images/history/GatoPajero.jpg" class="img-thumbnail" />
			<figcaption>The namesake: felis pajero</figcaption>
		</figure>
		<p>
			The off-road vehicle we all know by the
			name Pajero owes its name to a South American wildcat, the wild feline
			<em>felis pajero</em>. The press release issued by Mitsubishi read as follows:
			“The <em>felis pajero </em>is a wildcat of short, powerful build, famous
			for its magnificently patterned coat. It lives in the rugged regions
			of Patagonia in South America. In the manner of wild cats, felis pajero combines strength and grace
			into a harmonious whole and prefers rough terrain that other animals
			avoid. Felis pajero naturally embodies the conquering spirit of the Pajero,
			the newest model from Mitsubishi.” A true Pajero fan seems to have worked on that press
			release, for it certainly speaks from the heart of every enthusiast.
		</p>
	</div>
